 "header-quote": "Presented in milligrams per 100 grams (mg/100g) of sample on a fresh-weight (FW) basis. USDA Analytical Data and Published Literature (North America)",
 "molecular-weight": "The workbook states a molecular weight per compound on its own row. Glucoraphanin is 437 g/mol, which is the free acid. This is the first glucosinolate source here that states its convention, and it is why these figures can be held in milligrams at all: every other candidate reports umol and leaves the mass to be guessed.",
 "method": "HPLC to ISO 9167-1 (2019) for the USDA analytical data; the rest is published North American literature compiled by USDA",
 "component": "glucoraphanin only. The release carries 31 glucosinolates and the page has a column for one of them plus a total.",
 "why-a-range": "A food maps to many rows because the release samples cultivars, growing locations and storage regimes rather than foods, the same shape as the FAO phytate release. No single row is the food, so the cell is a range over the means and the observations are kept below.",
 "not-mapped": "Rocket reaches nothing: the release has arugula only as microgreens. Turnip, turnip greens, pak choi and watercress have rows but no glucoraphanin figure. Cauliflower and swede have no cooked entry, and red cabbage no raw one beyond the single chopped row used here.",
